; expect 127
; MAC-LANG-15: a macro rule's parameter is a PATTERN (2026-08-16).
;
; Until this commit a rule head was a flat list of distinct bare
; identifiers and the invocation's ARGUMENT COUNT chose the rule.
; `parseIdentList` refused anything else at the pattern's own paren -
; `AX2001 expected identifier, found `(`` - which is what the compiler
; before this commit answers for this whole file.
;
; Four of MAC-LANG-15's six pattern kinds land here. The two that do
; not are named because each is blocked on something real, not on
; effort:
;
;   `(p ...)`             ellipsis, MAC-LANG-16, which needs `...` to
;                         lex - `.` is `TK_DOT` and three of them are
;                         three tokens
;   a LITERAL IDENTIFIER  MAC-LANG-17, which needs scope sets: a
;                         literal identifier matches by BINDING and
;                         renaming has nothing to compare a binding
;                         against
;
; The second is why the head of a nested pattern here is a BINDER and
; not a keyword. `(shape (h T))` matches `(a Two)` AND `(a b Two)`
; would match `(shape (h g T))` - what discriminates is the form's
; SHAPE, never the head's spelling. Measured, because the first draft
; of this fixture assumed otherwise: `((defOp (unary T)) ...)` and
; `((defOp (binary T)) ...)` both matched `(defOp (binary E))`, the
; first rule won, and the file answered 2 where it expected 3. So the
; discriminating powers this commit ships are exactly two - the shape
; of a nested form, and the value of a literal - and the spec's own
; `simplify` table (§10.4), whose rules are told apart by `+` and `*`
; in head position, still needs MAC-LANG-17.
;
; Three more limits, each the PARSER showing through a design that
; keeps pattern-ness positional (MAC-LANG-14a), and each measured
; rather than reasoned:
;
;   ((f x) y)   is the same tree as (f x y) - a spine does not record
;               where its parens were. Symmetric, so matching stays
;               consistent, but two RULES differing only that way are
;               one rule and the second dies unreported
;   (p)         is the bare binder `p`, because a one-element form
;               loses its parens on the ARGUMENT side too. So a rule
;               written `((m (x)) ...)` matches everything of arity
;               one, and AX3033 says so by name rather than telling
;               its author to write the parens they already wrote
;   (if a b)    and `let`, `lambda`, `match` - a keyword-headed form
;               arrives under its OWN tag, not as a spine, so no
;               parenthesised pattern matches one. Such an argument
;               falls through to a plain binder
;
; A literal compares its VALUE and not its spelling, in every kind:
; `10` matches `1_0`, `1.0` matches `1.00`, `"\t"` matches a real tab.
; The first two are free - the parser decodes them - and the third is
; not, because a string node keeps its lexeme verbatim; the matcher
; decodes as it compares. An earlier revision did not, and refused the
; tab.
;
; Seven claims, one bit each (127 when all hold):
;
;    1  a nested TWO-element form selects the two-element rule
;    2  a nested THREE-element form selects the three-element rule -
;       and both rules take ONE argument, which is the case the
;       previous refusal forbade outright (`AX3020`, "declares two
;       rules taking 1 arguments"). Ablated - delete the
;       three-element rule - the file does not answer 125 but
;       REFUSES: the invocation falls through to the bare-identifier
;       rule, `T` binds the whole form `(a b Three)`, and a
;       `syntax/join` over a form that is not a name is `AX3027`.
;       Which is a sharper claim than a wrong number, and was
;       measured rather than predicted
;    4  a bare identifier selects the identifier rule, so an
;       all-binder rule is still the default it always was
;    8  a LITERAL selects a rule: `(lit Zero 0)` takes the `0` rule.
;       Ablated - delete the `0` rule - this file answers 119, because
;       `(lit T n)` then binds `n` to `0` and reports 0
;   16  a binder in the OTHER rule of that pair still binds: `n` is
;       64 and reaches the template
;   32  a binder INSIDE a nested pattern reaches the template as a
;       NAME - `(named (nm T))` invoked as `(named (pick Two))`
;       generates `pickTwo`, so `syntax/join` composes with a pattern
;       binder and not only with a parameter
;   64  `_` matches and binds NOTHING, and two of them in one rule are
;       legal where two of any other name are `AX3020`. Before this
;       commit `_` was an ordinary identifier: `((hole T _ _) ...)`
;       drew two AX3020s, measured
;
; Selection is MAC-LANG-18's: the rules are tried IN ORDER and the
; first whose pattern matches wins. `tests/diagnostics/600` pins what
; that costs - an all-binder rule matches everything of its arity, so
; a later rule of that arity can never run and is refused at the
; macro's own line.
(data Two (T2))

(data Three (T3))

(data Bare (TB))

(data Zero (Z0))

(data Some (S1))

;; three rules, ALL of arity one, told apart by the shape of the form
(macro shape ((shape (h T))
   (:: (syntax/join s T) (-> T Int))
   (fn ((syntax/join s T) v) 1))
  ((shape (h g T))
   (:: (syntax/join s T) (-> T Int))
   (fn ((syntax/join s T) v) 2))
  ((shape T)
   (:: (syntax/join s T) (-> T Int))
   (fn ((syntax/join s T) v) 4)))

;; two rules of arity two, told apart by a LITERAL
(macro lit ((lit T 0)
   (:: (syntax/join l T) (-> T Int))
   (fn ((syntax/join l T) v) 8))
  ((lit T n)
   (:: (syntax/join l T) (-> T Int))
   (fn ((syntax/join l T) v) n)))

;; a binder inside a nested pattern, used as a NAME
(macro named ((named (nm T))
   (:: (syntax/join nm T) (-> T Int))
   (fn ((syntax/join nm T) v) 16)))

;; two wildcards in one rule
(macro hole ((hole T _ _)
   (:: (syntax/join h T) (-> T Int))
   (fn ((syntax/join h T) v) 32)))

(shape (a Two))

(shape (a b Three))

(shape Bare)

(lit Zero 0)

(lit Some 64)

(named (pick Two))

(hole Three 9 9)

(:: b2i (-> Bool Int))

(fn (b2i x)
  (if x
    1
    0
  )
)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (+ (b2i (== (sTwo T2) 1)) (+ (* 2 (b2i (== (sThree T3) 2))) (+ (* 4 (b2i (== (sBare TB) 4))) (+ (* 8 (b2i (== (lZero Z0) 8))) (+ (* 16 (b2i (== (lSome S1) 64))) (+ (* 32 (b2i (== (pickTwo T2) 16))) (* 64 (b2i (== (hThree T3) 32))))))))))
